<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Fully Aware daily-scan LaunchAgent: run the Codex-managed scan pipeline every
  day at 06:15 local -- 30 minutes after the 05:45 boot-pack agent, so the pack
  the scanner reads is already fresh (stage 0 re-runs morning-pack.sh only if it
  somehow is not).

  The pipeline is Codex scan -> Fable review -> Codex summarize, all three stages
  behind their own watchdogs, degrading rather than aborting. It always exits 0:
  a partial run is reported in state/logs/daily-scan.log and the per-stage
  .FAILED markers, never as a launchd failure.

  /bin/bash -lc is deliberate. The runner shells out to `codex`, `claude`, `gh`
  and `git`; launchd hands the agent a stripped PATH, and a login shell gives it
  the same environment Anthony's own terminal has. (run-daily-scan.sh also sets
  an explicit PATH itself -- belt and braces, same lesson as the SAGA agents.)
  `gh` matters here: the sandboxed scanner is offline, so the RUNNER collects
  open-PR state and feeds it to the model as data.

  UNARMED until Anthony merges: this plist points at code on the
  feat/codex-daily-scan branch. Install + load with
  tools/daily-scan/install-daily-scan-launchagent.sh AFTER merge to main. Do not
  load it against unmerged code.

  D30 discipline: every model stage runs Codex under a read-only sandbox, and
  the runner's own writes all land in the gitignored state/ tree (scan, review,
  brief, LATEST.md, thread-id, raw logs, transient prompt inputs). Two side
  effects sit outside it: stage 0 may re-run tools/morning-pack.sh, which
  rewrites the boot pack, and the codex CLI keeps session rollouts under
  ~/.codex. Nothing in this lane commits or pushes.

  A single run is serialised by an atomic mkdir lock at state/daily-scan/.lock,
  whose pid file names the owner. If that pid is alive when this job fires, the
  new run logs and exits 0 rather than racing it. If the owner died without
  releasing (kill -9, reboot), the firing run CLEARS the lock and still exits
  without scanning -- the next fire, or a second manual run, does the scan. The
  breaker is never the runner; that is what holds an 8-way race to one winner.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.anthonyflores.fully-aware.daily-scan</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-lc</string>
        <string>cd /Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware &amp;&amp; tools/daily-scan/run-daily-scan.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware</string>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Hour</key>
        <integer>6</integer>
        <key>Minute</key>
        <integer>15</integer>
    </dict>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware/state/logs/daily-scan.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware/state/logs/daily-scan.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
